## 3. Public Key Certificate Pinning (Anti-MITM)

Public Key Pinning validates the cryptographic fingerprint (SHA-256 of the Subject Public Key Info) of the server certificate. Even if a compromised internal Corporate Proxy or rogue Certificate Authority (CA) injects a trusted root certificate onto the device, the connection is instantly severed:

### Rule: Always Pin with at least Two Backup Pins
Never pin a single certificate hash. If a production certificate expires or is revoked in an emergency, having pre-generated backup pins ensures that new certificates can be deployed without bricking the mobile app.

<Tabs>
  <TabItem label="Android (Network Security Config)" icon="seti:xml">

### Declarative Pinning in `network_security_config.xml`
Create `android/app/src/main/res/xml/network_security_config.xml`:

```xml
<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<network-security-config>
    <!-- Disable cleartext HTTP entirely -->
    <base-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="false">
        <trust-anchors>
            <certificates src="system" />
        </trust-anchors>
    </base-config>

    <!-- Pin Enterprise API Gateway Domains -->
    <domain-config>
        <domain includeSubdomains="true">api.enterprise.internal</domain>
        <domain includeSubdomains="true">gateway.enterprise.internal</domain>
        <pin-set expiration="2027-12-31">
            <!-- Primary Production Certificate Public Key -->
            <pin digest="SHA-256">WoiWRyIOVNa9ihaBciRSC7XHjliYS9VwUGOIud4PB18=</pin>
            <!-- Disaster Recovery Backup Pin 1 -->
            <pin digest="SHA-256">k2ErWTSpFPuzfup+ge38Rn9S8SXVoECp3gBCK1QTdTU=</pin>
            <!-- Disaster Recovery Backup Pin 2 -->
            <pin digest="SHA-256">r/mIkG3eEpVdm+u/ko/cwxzOMo1bk4TyHIlByibiA5E=</pin>
        </pin-set>
    </domain-config>
</network-security-config>
```

Link the config in `android/app/src/main/AndroidManifest.xml`:
```xml
<application
    android:networkSecurityConfig="@xml/network_security_config"
    ... >
